{"name":"Honey Ginger","hex":"#ba6219","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sweet, viscous, gold-colored fluid produced from plant nectar by bees, and often consumed by humans.","A variety of this substance."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Involving or resembling honey.","Of a pale yellow to brownish-yellow color, like most types of honey."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey"},{"term":"ginger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The pungent aromatic rhizome of a tropical Asian herb, Zingiber officinale, used as a spice and as a stimulant and acarminative.","The plant that produces this rhizome."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a reddish-brown color.","Having hair or fur of this color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginger"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger","wikidata":"Q35625"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/500px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/1280px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/500px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French honey from different floral sources, with visible differences in color and texture","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiels_d'Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg/500px-Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg/500px-Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A honey bee with its proboscis extended into a calyx of goldenrod","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABee_on_-calyx_935.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Honey_comb.jpg/500px-Honey_comb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/H